{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"set-multiset","title":"set & multiset","group":"STL Containers & Iterators","order":51,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>std::set</code> keeps unique keys in sorted order (a balanced binary search tree); <code>std::multiset</code> allows duplicates. Everything is O(log n).</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circle-dot\"></i><code>std::set</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;set&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::set&lt;int&gt; s{5, 1, 4, 1, 3};              // duplicates dropped, sorted\n    for (int x : s) std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    auto [it, inserted] = s.insert(4);            // already there\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; inserted &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *it &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; s.count(3) &lt;&lt; s.contains(9) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // contains: C++20\n    s.erase(1);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *s.begin() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *s.rbegin() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; s.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // min, max\n\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *s.lower_bound(4) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *s.upper_bound(4) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // first &gt;= 4, first &gt; 4\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 3 4 5\n0 4\n10\n3 5 3\n4 5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Custom order</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;functional&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;set&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nstruct ByLength {\n    bool operator()(const std::string&amp; a, const std::string&amp; b) const {\n        return a.size() != b.size() ? a.size() &lt; b.size() : a &lt; b;   // tie-break so distinct words stay distinct\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::set&lt;int, std::greater&lt;int&gt;&gt; desc{3, 1, 2};\n    for (int x : desc) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::set&lt;std::string, ByLength&gt; words{\"banana\", \"fig\", \"apple\", \"kiwi\", \"pear\"};\n    for (const auto&amp; w : words) std::cout &lt;&lt; w &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>321\nfig kiwi pear apple banana</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Set elements are <code>const</code> — you can't modify a key in place (it would break the ordering). Erase and re-insert, or use C++17 <code>extract</code> to modify a node's key without reallocating.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circles\"></i><code>std::multiset</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;set&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::multiset&lt;int&gt; ms{3, 1, 3, 2, 3};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; ms.count(3) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; ms.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    ms.erase(ms.find(3));           // erase ONE copy\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; ms.count(3) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    ms.erase(3);                    // erase ALL copies\n    for (int x : ms) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 5\n2\n12</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">DSA use</div><p>A <code>multiset</code> works as a <em>sliding-window sorted bag</em>: insert/erase in O(log n) and read the min/max from <code>begin()</code>/<code>rbegin()</code> — handy for “median / max of the last k elements” problems.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How is std::set implemented and what are its complexities?</summary><div><p>As a self-balancing binary search tree (a red-black tree in all major implementations). Insert, erase and find are O(log n); iteration visits keys in sorted order; <code>lower_bound</code>/<code>upper_bound</code> answer “first key ≥ / &gt; x” in O(log n).</p></div></details></section>"}